WEBVTT

NOTE English track. Translated from the slides and burned-in captions. Timings are approximate.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.500
How to use the EMI knot pusher
EMICS150 and 150C

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.300
Toshiaki Ito, Japanese Red Cross
Nagoya Daiichi Hospital

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.000
Features of the EMICS 150
A round head that avoids catching nearby
sutures or chordae when lifted

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.500
A pin-type suture catcher that spares the thread
A deep, mirror-polished groove with low friction

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:29.500
Holding the threads (right-handed)
Not crossing them causes less confusion

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.500
Make a loop
1. Make one loop one-handed with the left hand

7
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.500
2. Hand it to the assistant for gentle traction
This thread becomes the "axis thread"

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Load the thread on the knot pusher
Seat the thread in the suture catcher groove

9
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.500
Rest the left index finger on the knot
The assistant keeps traction on the right thread

10
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Rotate the knot pusher clockwise

11
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.500
Step 1
Before lowering the knot pusher,
pinch the axis thread with thumb and index

12
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:09.500
Apply even tension to both threads
and seat them firmly in the groove
The assistant only holds lightly

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Step 2
Tilting the wrist radially slackens
the non-axis thread slightly

14
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.500
Lowering the knot pusher in this state
slides smoothly (tilting wrist technique)

15
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.500
Step 3
Return the wrist. Push the knot pusher

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.500
Return
The assistant keeps gentle traction

17
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.500
Second loop
Make a loop one-handed in the same direction
as the first (granny knot)

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:59.500
Repeat step 1
Pinch the axis thread with your fingers
before lowering the knot pusher

19
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
On the second throw, pinch the axis thread
with thumb and index and pull firmly

20
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.500
Tilt the wrist radially and pull the axis thread
to tighten. An application of the slip knot technique

21
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:39.500
The principle of the slip knot technique

22
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:49.500
Third throw: make a loop one-handed
the opposite way from the first (square knot)

23
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:59.500
Step 2
Tilting the wrist radially slackens
the non-axis thread slightly

24
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:09.500
Whose force tightens the knot?
Not the assistant's traction. The surgeon holds
both threads in the left hand and tightens

25
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:19.500
Use the pushing force of the knot pusher
as counter-traction so the tissue
is not pulled and torn

26
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:29.500
Tightening from the fourth throw on
Pull the axis thread slightly less than the left,
tilt the left wrist ulnarly and tighten evenly

27
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:39.500
Pitfall
Avoid a throw that merely wraps around
the axis thread

28
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:49.500
Pitfall
Pulling the left thread too hard locks the knot
No capital "Y"

29
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:59.500
How to form and pull each throw
Pull the axis thread hard on throws 1 to 3,
the left thread on throws 4 to 6

30
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.500
Summary 1: sending the knot smoothly
The assistant's only job is steady, light
traction on the axis thread

31
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.500
The surgeon holds both threads in the left hand,
seats them, applies even tension, tilts the wrist
to slacken the non-axis thread, then pushes

32
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.500
Summary 2: making a firm knot
Form the knot at the base of the axis thread
On the second throw (granny), pull the axis thread hard

33
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.500
The surgeon controls the tightening with both threads
From the fourth throw, angle the left wrist
to pull the left thread too

34
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:44.500
How to use the EMICS150 (half speed)

35
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.500
Hand the right thread to the assistant
as the axis thread

36
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:59.500
Load the thread on the knot pusher pin

37
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.500
Rotate 180 degrees to the right

38
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.500
Pinch the axis thread with the left thumb and index
and seat the threads firmly in both grooves

39
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.500
Lower the knot pusher

40
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.500
Make the second throw in the same direction

41
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:29.500
Tighten while tilting the left wrist radially

42
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:39.500
Tilt the wrist slightly radially

43
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.500
Tilt the wrist and lower the knot

44
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.500
The handle is released

45
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.500
It slides down under its own weight

46
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.500
Tilting the wrist slackens the non-axis thread
very slightly

47
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.500
Overdoing it lets the thread slip out of the groove

48
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:14.500
The tilting wrist technique solves
the knot pusher's common troubles

49
00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.500
The thread slips out of the groove
Seating it first before pushing keeps it in

50
00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:24.000
The thread catches and the knot is hard to lower
The wrist tilt gives the non-axis thread just enough slack

51
00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.500
The assistant pulls the axis thread and shortens the other side
The surgeon controls the axis thread from the start

52
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:39.500
Structure of the EMICS-150C
A small round head with a closed,
pin-type suture catcher

53
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:49.500
Pass the thread through the suture catcher

54
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:59.500
Step 1
Pinch the axis thread before lowering the pusher
The assistant only holds lightly

55
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:09.500
Step 2
Tilting the wrist radially slackens
the non-axis thread slightly

56
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:19.500
Step 3
Return the wrist
Pull both threads in balance and push the knot pusher

57
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.500
Pitfalls and troubleshooting
Slippery, supple thread: good
High-friction thread that resists loosening: caution

58
00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.500
If the knot locks while still loose,
switch the axis thread and tighten again

59
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:39.500
Avoid a loose knot

60
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:48.000
A firm radial wrist tilt pulls the axis thread
and tightens the knot naturally
Press the knot pusher firmly

61
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Always know which thread is the axis
When pulling toward the wound,
the shallower, nearer thread is the axis

62
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:07.500
How to choose the axis thread
Between thread A in front and thread B from behind,
the axis thread is A

63
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:14.000
(End)
